\contentsline {section}{: If your company uses genuine Office, this is the best choice. Format compatibility with the desktop version is nearly perfect, avoiding the slight layout shifts that WPS may have with some complex layouts. Deeply integrated with OneDrive, the document you were halfway through editing on your computer opens as the latest version on your phone.}{75}{section*.23}%
\contentsline {section}{: WeChat's “Float” feature is actually a hidden gem. When you see a good article but don't have time to read it carefully, tap “Float” in the upper-right corner, and it shrinks into a small dot attached to the edge of the screen, ready to be tapped open to continue reading anytime. It's much more convenient than forwarding to “File Transfer Assistant”. In addition, read-later tools like Cubox or Pocket can collect good content you come across anywhere, across platforms.}{76}{section*.23}%
\contentsline {section}{: Many brands' cameras have a built-in “Document Scan” mode. If you can't find it, download Microsoft Lens from the app store (free, and integrated with OneDrive).}{77}{section*.23}%
\contentsline {section}{: If you use a Windows PC + Office 365, OneDrive is a natural match. Save a document in Word, and it's automatically stored in the cloud. The 1TB space bundled with the Office subscription is a great deal. The mobile app's auto photo backup feature is also very handy.}{77}{section*.23}%
\contentsline {section}{The client raised a second round of comments, so I used WPS's brush tool to directly circle the revision positions on the PDF, with voice annotations explaining the revision approach.}{78}{section*.23}%
