\contentsline {section}{: Microsoft has an app called “Link to Windows”, built into Samsung phones, and other Android phones can download it from the app store. After pairing, you can directly view phone notifications, reply to texts, browse photos, and even make and receive calls on your computer. I've used it for two years—during meetings I don't have to keep looking down at my phone; a notification popping up on the computer screen is enough.}{149}{section*.40}%
\contentsline {section}{: Each brand calls it differently—Huawei calls it “Wireless Projection”, Xiaomi calls it “Cast”, OPPO/vivo calls it “Multi-Screen Interaction”. Usually findable in the quick settings panel; if not, search “screen cast” in Settings.}{150}{section*.40}%
\contentsline {section}{: The watch monitors heart rate, blood oxygen, sleep, and steps 24 hours a day, and this data is automatically aggregated into the health app on your phone. Over time, you can see your trends—have you been walking too little this month, has your sleep quality improved this week.}{150}{section*.40}%
